          { name: "Middle-Class Household Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $80,409 median household income with 26.4% renters and 28.8% college education, AL-HD-5 is a middle-class constituency materially concerned with housing affordability, healthcare costs, education quality, and economic mobility for their children. These are not abstract policy concerns—they are household budget pressures that affect how families evaluate their representative's performance. A legislative record specific to these concerns (housing policy, healthcare access, education funding) provides both effective governing and the strongest political defense against Democratic challenge on the economy." },
          { name: "Unopposed 2022 as Strategic Liability, Not Asset", tag: "lean-into", why: "Crawford's unopposed 2022 victory generated a 100% result that masks the district's actual competitive terrain. The 13.13% Black and 7.49% Hispanic populations, combined with 26.4% renters and college-educated younger voters, represent constituencies capable of generating substantial Democratic turnout if mobilized by a credible challenger focused on household economics. The absence of a 2022 opponent means there is no public legislative record to defend or point to—a future challenger will define the incumbent's record from scratch, making proactive legislative accomplishment on household concerns the most important hedge against that dynamic." },
          { name: "Minority Community Engagement (Black and Hispanic)", tag: "careful", why: "At 13.13% Black and 7.49% Hispanic, these communities represent over 20% of the district—a non-trivial swing constituency in a district with no recent competitive election to test their actual partisan preference or mobilization capacity. The unopposed 2022 result provides no information about whether these communities are persuadable, abstaining, or already mobilized Republican—direct constituent service, visible legislative attention to their economic and social concerns, and authentic relationship-building are necessary to prevent their mobilization by a future Democratic challenger." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Victory as Evidence of a Safe Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% result in 2022 reflects lack of Democratic competition, not necessarily strong district alignment or insurmountable Republican advantage. The demographic profile—middle-class, college-educated, 26% renters, 20%+ minority—is precisely the terrain where Democrats have shown capacity to compete in similar districts. Any incumbent who assumes the unopposed result indicates a safe seat and does not build a visible legislative record of household-economic delivery is making a strategic error that a well-resourced challenger can exploit." },
          { name: "Overlooking the College-Educated Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 28.8% college attainment in a $80K middle-class district, the college-educated population is significant and increasingly swing-prone on healthcare, education, and economic-mobility issues. A legislator who campaigns exclusively to the white working-class homeowner base and neglects the college-educated professional constituency—particularly younger college-educated adults and younger families—concedes a persuadable demographic to potential Democratic challenge on exactly the economic and social concerns that matter most to educated professionals." }
