          { name: "Black Community Education & Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 21.97%, the Black community in Al-HD-10 is nearly one-quarter of the district and large enough to be swing-decisive in a 4-point race. Education funding, workforce development, economic opportunity in growing sectors, and equitable service delivery are the issues most likely to activate Black voter participation and candidate preference. A representative who limits engagement to periodic constituent service, rather than addressing these structural economic concerns, creates an opening for opposition mobilization." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency & Ticket-Splitting", tag: "careful", why: "The 50.4% college-attainment rate describes a constituency of professionals and service workers who are educated enough to evaluate candidates across multiple issues rather than default to inherited partisan loyalty. This group often prioritizes education quality, environmental stewardship, and social service access alongside fiscal conservatism, and they will split tickets if they perceive a candidate is neglecting their priorities. A representative who takes this constituency's education and values seriously, rather than assuming their income level guarantees Republican voting, builds durable support." },
          { name: "Treating 52% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result in Oklahoma is not a majority — it is a narrow plurality in a genuinely competitive district. Any incumbent who campaigns as though this margin represents a comfortable hold, rather than acknowledging that 48% actively chose someone else and that renters, Black voters, and young professionals are genuinely contested constituencies, is strategically vulnerable. The college education level and urban-adjacent character of this district mean voters are capable of sophisticated evaluation and responsive to evidence of representation." },
          { name: "Assuming Income Level Correlates with Conservative Policy Preference", tag: "avoid", why: "The $81,056 median household income is upper-middle-class, but in a college-educated district with 40.3% renters and meaningful communities of color, income alone does not determine policy preference. Young professionals with high incomes often prioritize education, environmental policy, and social service access; renters earning good wages still face housing affordability constraints; and Black and Hispanic households at all income levels evaluate candidates on whether they are addressed as constituents with specific policy needs rather than demographic categories. Defaulting to income-based assumptions about voting behavior risks missing the actual composition of this district." }
