          { name: "Rural Economic Development & Local Employment", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 15.7% college attainment, AL-HD-11's constituency relies on local employment opportunity and does not have the geographic mobility of a college-educated population — making rural economic development and workforce investment in non-college-track careers a direct constituent concern. State investment in rural infrastructure, support for small business, and training programs for manufacturing or skilled trades are the concrete policy levers that deliver tangible benefit to a working-class community. An incumbent who has secured visible economic development investment or workforce support demonstrates responsiveness to the district's primary economic vulnerability." },
          { name: "Unopposed 2022 Victory as a Strategic Vulnerability", tag: "careful", why: "An unopposed election provides no information about actual constituent satisfaction, opposition party recruitment capacity, or whether economic concerns have generated latent frustration. The 100% result is arithmetically real but strategically misleading — it may indicate either a genuinely durable Republican seat or a seat that appears safe only because no one has tested it. Any incumbent should treat an unopposed win as evidence of organizational weakness (no serious opposition mobilized), not strength, and should maintain heightened constituent service and economic responsiveness to prevent a credible challenger from repositioning the district as competitive." },
          { name: "Deploying Education Attainment as a Proxy for Voter Sophistication", tag: "avoid", why: "AL-HD-11's 15.7% college attainment is low, but this describes a working-class community with concrete economic stakes in their own households, not a population lacking capacity to evaluate economic governance. Messaging that treats low education as equivalent to low sophistication, or that substitutes cultural or identity-based appeals for answers to property tax and employment concerns, will read as dismissive and generate opposition from a constituency that is evaluating whether government delivers for them economically." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Homogeneity Guarantees Partisan Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "While AL-HD-11 is 86.14% white and overwhelmingly homeowner-dominant, an unopposed victory provides no evidence that these demographic characteristics translate into unshakeable partisan loyalty rather than conditional support based on economic performance. A challenger who offers specific answers to property tax burdens, healthcare costs, or rural employment opportunity could move a working-class constituency away from an incumbent perceived as unresponsive to material concerns. Demographic safety and political safety are distinct — the latter requires active constituent engagement, not passive demographic assumption." }
