          { name: "Taking 100% Electoral Results as Evidence of a Safe Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2022 unopposed result reflects the absence of a challenger, not the presence of an unshakeable electoral coalition — a critical distinction in a district this economically stressed. In rural western Oklahoma, a 43-year-old, $48,584-income constituency is actively evaluating whether current policy is delivering for them; a challenger with a concrete economic message and genuine rural engagement could find unexpected traction where the current 100% suggests a ceiling that is actually conditional on opposition weakness." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 27.6% Renter Population as an Electoral Afterthought", tag: "avoid", why: "One in four AL-HD-17 households are renters in a rural context where homeownership is typically the majority tenure — a signal of real housing stress and cost-of-living pressure that deserves policy attention. Any candidate who focuses exclusively on the homeowner majority and treats renters as a negligible constituency misses both a material governance obligation and a latent electoral opportunity, particularly if a challenger offers specific policy remedies for housing cost relief or rental market regulation that this constituency evaluates as more responsive than incumbent indifference." }
