          { name: "Education Quality and Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 37.8 median age and 58.8% college attainment together signal a constituency of working-age professionals evaluating school quality for their own children and higher education costs for themselves and their families. K-12 funding adequacy, teacher compensation, and the affordability of four-year degrees are not policy abstractions but lived experiences that determine whether this constituency stays in the district and builds long-term community ties. Any candidate who demonstrates specific knowledge of the district's school funding trajectory and concrete plans to address higher education costs is credibly speaking to the primary quality-of-life concern of this educated, 37.8-year-old demographic." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap & Minority Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The ~5.4% gap between reported Census categories and 100% in a district this affluent and educated likely represents Asian, Native American, or other minority professionals whose economic interests align with the broader educated constituency but whose civic engagement patterns and specific policy priorities (diversity, inclusion, immigrant pathway, Native American governance) may differ from the white majority. In a 58-42 district, treating this ~5.4% as an active constituent community rather than a demographic footnote is both a governance obligation and a latent electoral opportunity; ignoring it means governing less than the full community and leaving swing votes on the table." },
          { name: "Assuming 58% Represents Durable Republican Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "A 58% result in a college-educated, $108k-income suburban district is not a mandate—it is a plurality of a constituency where education level is the strongest predictor of ticket-splitting and issue-based voting. College-educated voters are the least loyal to inherited party affiliation and the most responsive to governance performance and specific policy credibility. Any candidate who campaigns the 58% as though it represents a stable floor and consolidates only the existing Republican base, without addressing the 42% opposition's driving concerns, is misreading the electoral fundamentals of an educated suburban constituency." },
          { name: "Treating Affluence as Equivalent to Partisan Security", tag: "avoid", why: "AL-HD-25's $108,391 median income and 58.8% college attainment make it prosperous but not necessarily Republican-secure. Affluent, educated suburban constituencies are the first to abandon incumbents who fail on specific economic performance (healthcare costs, education funding, infrastructure) and the most capable of organizing alternative candidates. The 28.7% renter rate adds volatility: younger, mobile professionals in high-income suburbs are precisely the demographic most likely to swing if they perceive a governance failure. Wealth without partisan loyalty is a warning sign, not an all-clear." }
