          { name: "Unopposed Status as Temporary, Not Permanent", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 2022 unopposed result reflects the absence of Democratic recruitment and activism, not genuine consensus across all demographic groups in the district. The 15.49% Black population and ~3.8% Native American residents did not contest the seat in the last cycle, but this should be treated as latent rather than resolved opposition. Any candidate who assumes unopposed status indicates durable support and focuses legislative effort only on consolidating the white homeowner majority risks leaving the constituency gaps open for Democratic recruitment in a future cycle when the national political environment shifts or when local organizing improves." },
          { name: "Black Community Engagement & Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "At 15.49% of the district, the Black community is a meaningful constituency whose economic concerns — educational opportunity, healthcare access, workforce development — are real and distinct from the white homeowner majority's focus on property tax management. The absence of visible Black representation in current district politics does not mean these communities lack governance needs; it reflects the current imbalance of power and attention. Active constituent service on education funding, healthcare access in predominantly Black neighborhoods, and economic opportunity requires genuine engagement, not token acknowledgment, and should be treated as a political investment that prevents future opposition consolidation." },
          { name: "Treating 100% 2022 Result as Reflecting Actual Voter Consensus", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election does not mean 100% of the constituency supports the incumbent or is satisfied with current representation — it means Democrats did not field a candidate and/or voter turnout reflected the absence of competition. The white homeowner majority likely did vote Republican, but the Black and Native American communities' non-participation should not be read as consent. Campaigning as though unopposed status reflects genuine consensus across all demographic groups ignores the real possibility of future opposition consolidation if economic conditions worsen or if Democratic recruitment improves." },
          { name: "Ignoring Economic Vulnerability in the $70,984 Income Band", tag: "avoid", why: "Middle-income homeowners at $70,984 are experiencing real cost pressures that are often invisible in partisan narratives focused on either poverty or wealth inequality, but which are materially significant for household financial security. Property tax increases, healthcare cost growth, and service delivery inflation hit this income band harder than higher-income households and create genuine economic anxiety despite appearing solidly middle-class on the surface. A representative who dismisses these concerns as politically secondary or treats them as secondary to tax-cutting ideology rather than cost-management governance is misreading the material reality of constituent life and creating opening for opposition." }
