          { name: "Affluent Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "With $105,539 median income, 76% homeownership (implied), and a 43.3 median age, HD-43 is a constituency of asset-holding professionals for whom property values, tax fairness, and economic stability are primary concerns. This is the demographic group most responsive to messaging about tax burden, regulatory burden on business, and protection of property rights — the core of a durable 74% Republican margin. A candidate who can demonstrate concrete delivery on tax fairness and property-rights protection consolidates this base while freeing political energy to engage persuadable college-educated voters on higher-order policy concerns." },
          { name: "Demographic Stability & Electoral Overconfidence", tag: "careful", why: "The 77.07% white, affluent, college-educated profile of HD-43 appears demographically stable, but the district's actual competitive vulnerability is not demographic change — it is issue-driven persuasion of the college-educated professionals who already live here. A candidate who treats the 74% margin as demographically inevitable rather than actively defended is vulnerable to sustained Democratic messaging on education-correlated issues like healthcare, environment, or governance norms. The district's demographic stability actually increases the importance of issue-specific engagement, since the opponent base is not geographic or demographic but ideological." },
          { name: "Treating College-Educated Voters as an Ideological Write-Off", tag: "avoid", why: "Any Republican strategy that cedes college-educated professionals to Democrats rather than contesting them directly on specific policy grounds will lose margin in this district over time. HD-43 voters at the 63.9% college-attainment level can be persuaded on healthcare, environment, or governance if offered genuinely competitive Republican solutions; they cannot be persuaded if the candidate's message treats education itself as a liability or colleges as enemy institutions. This messaging error is both strategically losing and actively offensive to the district's demographic majority." },
          { name: "Assuming the 26% Opposition is Unchangeable", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 63.9% college attainment and $105,539 median income, the 26% Democratic share is not a monolithic bloc driven by demographic determinism — it is a persuadable group of educated professionals with specific policy concerns around healthcare, environment, or institutional governance. Any candidate who approaches the 26% as an irreducible opposition rather than a set of voters to be engaged on their actual concerns is leaving electoral margin on the table. The college-educated professional who votes Democratic because they genuinely believe Democratic healthcare policy is superior is more persuadable through policy argument than through demographic or ideological dismissal." }
