          { name: "Housing Affordability & Cost-of-Living Pressure in an Urban/Suburban Context", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 41.9% renter rate is substantially higher than rural Oklahoma averages and signals a constituency for whom housing affordability, rental protections, and the relationship between income and housing cost are active economic concerns. At $79,434 median household income and 36.8 median age, this is a district of working-age adults and younger families managing real affordability pressure in what is likely an appreciating real estate market. A legislative agenda that addresses zoning flexibility, rental market regulation, property tax fairness, and first-time homebuyer support is more credible and resonant than abstract conservative positioning on housing as a market-only issue." },
          { name: "Hispanic and Asian Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "Hispanic and Asian residents together represent 11.41% of the district—a meaningful and growing demographic that is currently undercounted in turnout relative to its population share. In college-educated, urban/suburban districts, Hispanic and Asian voters tend to be economically mobile and professionally oriented, with direct concerns about education quality, immigration policy clarity, and whether government serves as an obstacle or facilitator of economic opportunity. This constituency requires specific candidate engagement on its material concerns; passive assumption of their support or conflation with a generic 'minority coalition' leaves the vote vulnerable to credible opposition." },
          { name: "Treating the 64% Margin as a Structural Stronghold", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-47's demographic profile—49.6% college-educated, high renter rate, young median age, significant and growing Black and Asian populations—does not naturally produce 64% Republican margins; these are typically volatile, persuadable constituencies that evaluate on candidate quality and specific policy responsiveness. The 64% result is earned through active constituent engagement, not guaranteed by partisan alignment. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though the margin is structurally secure and no opposition is credible is vulnerable to an organized challenger who articulates concrete answers to housing, education, and economic opportunity." },
          { name: "Ignoring or Tokenizing the Education Quality & Funding Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "At 49.6% college attainment—among the highest in the state—HD-47 has a constituency with direct and personal stakes in K-12 school quality, teacher compensation, and the education-to-opportunity pipeline. Generic rhetoric about 'supporting education' while opposing funding mechanisms or avoiding specificity on school quality and teacher pay is perceived as inauthentic by this constituency. A candidate who cannot articulate a detailed position on education funding, teacher compensation, and the relationship between education and economic opportunity in a college-educated district is vulnerable to a challenger who can." }
