          { name: "Public Education Quality and School System Responsiveness", tag: "lean-into", why: "A district with 41.4 median age and substantial renter and working-class populations depends on public education as the primary pathway to economic mobility for the next generation. School funding adequacy, teacher recruitment and retention, and school safety are the concrete issues that working-class parents evaluate their representative on. Demonstrating active legislative engagement with education funding and school system responsiveness is the way to signal that the representative understands what matters to the constituency's future." },
          { name: "Constituent Service Responsiveness and Visible Presence in Working-Class Neighborhoods", tag: "careful", why: "An 89% seat runs the risk of creating incumbent complacency where constituent services are concentrated in higher-income or commercial areas and neglected in working-class neighborhoods. The 33.5% renter rate and one-third working-class composition means that visible presence in neighborhood town halls, constituent service accessibility, and legislative visibility distributed across all income and residential areas are necessary to maintain institutional trust. Allowing constituent services to become perceived as concentrated in downtown or donor-friendly areas is the beginning of erosion." },
          { name: "Treating 89% as Permission to Govern Remotely or Prioritize Non-Constituent Interests", tag: "avoid", why: "The single greatest risk to an 89% incumbent is the assumption that such a commanding margin removes the obligation to remain actively constituent-responsive. In reality, it removes short-term electoral pressure and therefore increases the temptation to prioritize other interests — downtown development, donors, ideological positioning — over working-class constituent needs. The 89% is not permanent; it is the product of constituent trust, and that trust erodes when the representative becomes seen as serving interests other than the working-class base." },
          { name: "Neglecting Non-Black Minority Communities as Invisible in a Majority-Black District", tag: "avoid", why: "The 29.33% white, 1.68% Hispanic, and 0.5% Asian populations in AL-HD-60 are numerically small enough that they can easily become invisible in representation and constituent service allocation when the dominant focus is the 63.86% Black majority. Yet these communities have real economic and social concerns, and their perception of being neglected or underserved is the exact opening a challenger from another party could exploit to peel off a few percentage points and create a primary or general election opening. Inclusive constituent service across all demographic groups is both a governance obligation and an electoral safeguard." }
