          { name: "Public Education & Youth Opportunity Pipeline", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 18.5% college attainment rate—the lowest cohesion marker in the district—signals that public K-12 education quality and the pathway from graduation to economic opportunity are central constituent concerns in a majority-Black district where educational equity historically has been a governance priority. School funding adequacy, teacher compensation, and the concrete connection between education and employment outcomes are the mechanisms through which constituents evaluate whether government is investing in their children's future. Legislative leadership on education funding, workforce development partnerships, and accountability for school performance translates directly into constituent satisfaction in a district this education-conscious." },
          { name: "Unopposed Status & Primary Challenge Risk", tag: "careful", why: "The 100% result in 2022 reflects the absence of Republican opposition, not the absence of potential Democratic primary challenge or constituent dissatisfaction that could mobilize an alternative candidate. In a safe Democratic seat, the real competitive pressure comes from within-party challenges if an incumbent is perceived as unresponsive, invisible, or ineffective on the issues that matter to the working-class Black constituency. The incumbent or any Democratic candidate must treat this seat as requiring active constituent engagement and visible legislative achievement, not as having secured permanent incumbency merely by achieving 100% in an unopposed race." },
          { name: "Assuming Incumbent Permanence Without Continuous Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "Unopposed victory in 2022 does not translate into constitutional immunity from primary challenge if constituent frustration on economic security, education, or public safety reaches a threshold. Democratic primary voters in a 59.93% Black district will mobilize an alternative candidate if they conclude the incumbent is not delivering on material concerns or is invisible in the community. Any candidate who treats 100% as permission to reduce constituent engagement and focus on state-level positioning rather than district-level service delivery is creating the conditions for vulnerability." },
          { name: "Treating the Non-Black 40% as Irrelevant or Hostile", tag: "avoid", why: "With 35.23% white, 1.27% Hispanic, and 1.05% Asian constituents, HD-72 has meaningful minority populations (3.32% combined) alongside the white plurality whose material concerns and service needs should not be submerged into a majority-Black narrative or treated as outside the representative's direct responsibility. White working-class households at $46,799 income share the same economic pressures as Black households; housing affordability, job quality, and public service access are color-blind material concerns. Any candidate who signals through language, prioritization, or allocation that the non-Black 40% is secondary or hostile rather than a genuine constituent population is unnecessarily fragmenting what should be a unified working-class coalition." }
