        memoParagraphs: [
          "AL-HD-74 is a majority-Black district (60.05%) that produced a 60% Democratic result in 2022 — a result that on its surface appears to be a durable Democratic baseline in Alabama's partisan landscape. However, the district's economic profile reveals this is not a structural majority but a coalition dependent on continuous delivery to economically stressed households. The $53,335 median household income is below the state average, and critically, 45% of the district rents rather than owns — a renter plurality concentrated among Black households and younger adults for whom housing costs, wage levels, and economic mobility are survival-level concerns. The 31.9% college attainment rate and 37.5 median age signal a working-class constituency where education credentials are modest but upward mobility expectations are real. Any representative in this district is serving a community that evaluates on whether state government improves their economic conditions, not on partisan affiliation.",
          "The strategic vulnerability of a 60-40 result in this district is the inverse of its apparent strength. Because the Black constituency (60.05%) is the overwhelming source of Democratic votes, and because that Black constituency is concentrated in renter households at modest income, the district's political alignment is entirely dependent on whether that constituency believes government is delivering tangible economic improvements. If renter households face worsening housing affordability, if healthcare access or wage policy deteriorates, or if a challenger emerges offering more concrete economic solutions, the 60% coalition can fracture quickly. The 40% Republican opposition, meanwhile, includes a 31.61% white population in a state where white voting patterns are increasingly polarized and where economic messaging can shift white voter preferences. The real strategic challenge for any candidate in AL-HD-74 is not holding a comfortable Democratic seat — it is delivering economic results to a working-class, renter-heavy, economically vulnerable constituency that will evaluate the next election primarily on whether conditions have improved for them materially."
        ],
