          { name: "Education Policy & Higher Education Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "College-educated voters at 65.9% have direct stakes in both K-12 quality (if they have children) and higher education access and affordability (personal or family experience). An 81% Republican result in a highly educated district suggests the incumbent is effectively messaging on education policy; a challenger must offer a credible alternative on education funding, school quality metrics, and higher education affordability rather than competing for the same ground. The incumbent should emphasize concrete education outcomes—test scores, graduation rates, higher education access—and not assume educated voters automatically accept Republican framing without evidence of results." },
          { name: "Demographic-Electoral Alignment & Persuadability", tag: "careful", why: "The 81% Republican result in a district with 28.1 median age, 65.9% college education, and 44.1% renter rate is analytically unusual—these demographics typically correlate with greater ticket-splitting and Democratic lean in urban areas. This gap suggests either genuine ideological alignment or effective Republican messaging that has persuaded this constituency, but it also indicates a group that is evaluating based on specific material concerns rather than inherited partisan loyalty. An incumbent should recognize that while 81% is substantial, it is held conditionally; a challenger who credibly addresses young professionals' economic concerns could shift the alignment more easily than the raw margin suggests." },
          { name: "Treating 81% as Permanently Safe", tag: "avoid", why: "An 81% result in a district whose demographic profile (young, educated, urban, renting-heavy) typically produces more political contestation is not a permanent baseline—it is a held margin dependent on the incumbent's performance on education, healthcare, and economic mobility concerns. Any candidate who campaigns as if 81% guarantees re-election without directly addressing young professionals' material concerns risks allowing a credible challenger to reframe the race around education funding or healthcare access and reduce that margin significantly." },
          { name: "Assuming Educated Voters Are Ideologically Locked In", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated voters at 65.9% are ideologically diverse and evaluate candidates on competence and material policy delivery as much as ideology; an incumbent who relies on abstract conservative positioning without demonstrating concrete results on education, healthcare, and economic mobility may find educated professionals voting against them if they perceive policy failure. A challenger should not cede education and economic policy to the incumbent but instead offer specific, evidence-based alternatives that speak to how educated professionals evaluate governance." }
