          { name: "Lower-Income Housing Affordability and Renter Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 40.5% renters and $43,859 median household income, housing cost burden is a material daily reality for nearly half the district—and renters across racial lines share this economic concern. Candidates who speak with specificity to renter protections, property tax fairness that reduces landlord pass-throughs, and housing development that serves working-income families tap into a concrete constituency concern that can persuade voters across the district's racial composition. This issue cuts across the Black-white divide and appeals to the economic vulnerability of the district's lower-income base." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Affordability for Lower-Income Uninsured and Underinsured", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $43,859 median income and 19.1% college attainment, a substantial portion of HD-85's workforce lacks employer-sponsored healthcare or has high-deductible plans that function as barriers to care. Medicaid expansion, rural clinic access, and prescription drug cost relief are affirmative policy positions that resonate across the district's racial composition because the economic constraint is shared. Democratic messaging on healthcare expansion has historically mobilized lower-income voters; in a district this close, credible healthcare messaging to currently uninsured or cost-constrained households can shift 3-4 percentage points." },
          { name: "Racial Polarization and Black Voter Mobilization in a Nearly Balanced District", tag: "careful", why: "The 49.68% white and 44.13% Black population in a 54-46 Republican result indicates that Republican performance depends on either high white turnout or depressed Black turnout relative to the community's demographic composition. Any shift in Black voter mobilization—whether through improved turnout, candidate persuasion, or both—has significant margin impact in a district this narrow. Candidates must engage Black constituencies authentically around both economic concerns and any community-specific issues (criminal justice, education equity, employment discrimination) rather than assuming they are already consolidated; the 46% Democratic result suggests substantial capacity for improvement." },
          { name: "Running This District as a Safe Republican Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54-46 result in a district with a 44% Black population, 40.5% renters, and median income $6,000 below state averages is not a safe margin—it is a vulnerable configuration held by narrow white voter cohesion. Any incumbent who campaigns this district as though 54% represents a stable floor, rather than preparing for aggressive Democratic mobilization and persuasion efforts, is misreading the structural vulnerability. The opposition will target housing affordability and healthcare access; incumbents who treat these as secondary issues invite a competitive challenge." },
          { name: "Ignoring White Working-Class Economic Concerns in Favor of Abstract Conservative Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "White renters and lower-income white workers in HD-85—who comprise a portion of the 54% Republican coalition—face the same housing affordability and healthcare access constraints as their Black neighbors. Candidates who rely on cultural or partisan messaging rather than addressing these material economic concerns risk losing persuadable white working-class voters to a challenger who takes their economic security seriously. In a district this close, losing even 2-3 percentage points of white working-class support to defection or non-turnout is sufficient to flip the outcome." }
