          { name: "Defaulting to Abstract Conservative Economic Messaging Rather Than Specific Cost-of-Living Policy", tag: "avoid", why: "Working-class households at $60,605 income do not respond to abstract conservative economics — they respond to concrete policy on the material costs they face: healthcare, housing, and wages. A legislative record defined by opposition to regulation and defense of business interests, without specific answers to constituent cost-of-living concerns, is vulnerable to challenger framing that positions the incumbent as serving business interests rather than household economic security. Legislative specificity on healthcare affordability, housing policy, and wage-supporting economic development is more electorally durable than ideological consistency." }
