          { name: "Black Community Constituent Engagement and Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 25.65% Black population represents approximately one-quarter of AL-HD-104's residents and is a substantial constituency whose voting behavior is not fully captured by the 81% Republican margin. In Alabama's current political geography, Black voter participation and candidate preference remain responsive to specific legislative attention on economic mobility, criminal justice, healthcare access, and education — issues that overlap directly with working-class economic concerns. Any incumbent or challenger who treats the Black community as a peripheral constituency rather than as one-quarter of the district's core base is leaving material electoral capacity on the table and reducing the durability of the 81% result." },
          { name: "High Renter Rate and Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "At 36.9% renter rate, AL-HD-104 has a constituency whose housing costs are often their single largest expense and whose residential stability depends on wage growth outpacing rent increases — a constraint that has intensified across the South over the past decade. Housing policy is not typically a state-legislative priority in conservative districts, but the material reality of renter cost burdens in a $61,188-income district is significant. Any legislative response to housing affordability — whether through zoning, property tax fairness, or housing development incentives — requires careful framing to avoid alienating the homeowner-majority base while addressing the real constraints faced by the 36.9% who rent." },
          { name: "Treating the 81% Result as a Durable Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "An 81% result in a district with below-median income, 36.9% renter rate, and a substantial 25.65% Black population is not a supermajority reflecting durable constituent alignment — it is a result of base consolidation during a favorable partisan environment applied to a constituency whose material economic concerns require specific legislative attention to remain stable. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 81% represents a safe floor, rather than as a result that must be actively renewed through constituent service on economic security, will find the margin erode if a credible challenger emerges." },
          { name: "Abstract Conservative Messaging Without Material Constituent Results", tag: "avoid", why: "AL-HD-104's working-class profile — below-median income, high renter rate, non-college-educated majority — makes this a constituency that evaluates primarily on material legislative results, not ideological positioning or partisan loyalty. A representative whose record consists of conservative voting without concrete delivery on working-class economic concerns (wage growth, healthcare affordability, job training, housing stability) is vulnerable to a challenger who articulates specific legislative answers to these material pressures, particularly if that challenger can credibly engage both the working-class white and Black communities as a unified constituency." }
