          { name: "Over-Reliance on Partisan Messaging in Economic Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with median income of $65,888, high homeownership, and a working-class majority, voters evaluate their representative primarily on whether their household economic situation is improving — not on abstract partisan ideology or national political messaging. A legislative agenda grounded in partisan rhetorical opposition to the other party, rather than concrete answers to rural healthcare access, property tax fairness, and employment stability in a changing economy, will fail to consolidate the 10% opposition and risks allowing that 10% to grow into 15-20% if economic conditions deteriorate or a credible alternative emerges." }
