          { name: "Preventing Democratic Organizational Vacancy from Becoming Electoral Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "Unopposed victory in 2022 reflects Democratic weakness, not constituent satisfaction — a crucial distinction. In a district with modest income, limited educational attainment, and visible economic stress, an organized opposition campaign that speaks directly to working-class economic concerns can move this seat from 100% Republican to genuinely contested. The strategic imperative is to close the gap between the 100% electoral result and the material vulnerabilities beneath it by delivering measurably on rural economic development, not by assuming the 2022 outcome predicts 2024 or 2026." },
          { name: "AIAN Community Engagement & State Resource Delivery", tag: "careful", why: "The ~7% AIAN gap represents American Indian and Alaska Native residents whose presence in rural South Dakota is real and whose governance expectations deserve consistent attention. While this constituency is not large enough to determine the district's overall character, demonstrated respect for tribal sovereignty, transparent resource allocation to Native communities, and authentic partnership on issues affecting AIAN constituents distinguish good-faith governing from extractive politics. In a competitive election, how a representative has engaged the AIAN community becomes a visible measure of inclusive governance." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as a Reliable Electoral Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% win against no opposition is not a predictive indicator of electoral stability — it reflects organizational capacity, not voter preference. In a district where median income is $57,015 and college attainment is 16.8%, economic anxiety is a latent political fact. Any representative who campaigns as though 100% is a stable foundation and neglects the material concerns driving that anxiety will be vulnerable to a credible challenger who engages those concerns directly." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideology Over Rural Material Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "Rural South Dakota constituencies are pragmatic about governance: they evaluate on whether roads are maintained, healthcare is accessible, schools are funded, broadband works, and property taxes are fair — not on abstract partisan positioning. A legislative agenda that prioritizes partisan ideology over rural material concerns will alienate a constituency that is economically vulnerable and genuinely dependent on functional state government. This is a district where governing competence and constituent responsiveness are more electorally valuable than ideological purity." }
