          { name: "Aging Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 41.8-year median age, 79% homeownership, and $61,471 income, AL-SD-5's core constituency is aging faster than state and national averages, with fixed incomes under pressure from rising healthcare costs, property taxes, and rural service delivery expenses. Medicare sustainability, rural hospital viability, property tax fairness, and long-term care affordability are the material economic security concerns that shape how this constituency evaluates their representative. A legislator who engages these issues with specific policy solutions — not abstract ideology — demonstrates understanding of the actual pressures bearing on older homeowners' quality of life and asset preservation." },
          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access & Hospital Viability", tag: "lean-into", why: "Rural hospital closures and physician shortages across Alabama have accelerated over the past decade, creating immediate constituent problems for a district at 41.8-year median age where healthcare access and affordability are direct quality-of-life and survival issues. The $61,471 income means catastrophic healthcare costs are a genuine threat to household financial stability. A representative who engages local healthcare systems, state Medicaid policy, rural hospital funding, and physician recruitment with fluency and urgency demonstrates commitment to a core constituent vulnerability that is simultaneously a long-term demographic threat to the district's viability." },
          { name: "Youth Outmigration & Economic Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "The 17.9% college attainment rate — one of the lowest in this district sequence — reflects limited local pathways for young adults to build careers, which drives population outmigration and undermines long-term demographic stability. While not an immediate electoral threat to an incumbent with strong white homeowner support, demographic decline from persistent youth outmigration creates a 10- to 20-year vulnerability: the district becomes progressively older, smaller, and less economically diverse. A representative who acknowledges this challenge rather than ignoring it signals commitment to long-term community viability, not only current electoral safety." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Result as Invulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "Reed's unopposed 2022 win reflects the absence of organized Democratic challenge in a reliably Republican district, not invulnerability to a credible opposition candidate if one emerges. If demographic or redistricting changes reduce the Republican margin, or if a challenger builds genuine constituent relationships around economic security concerns the incumbent has neglected, the district could become genuinely competitive. An incumbent who assumes unopposed status guarantees perpetual safety — rather than consolidating constituent relationships and addressing material economic concerns — is building the conditions for a future vulnerability." },
          { name: "Engaging the 12.15% Non-White Population Actively", tag: "avoid", why: "At 6.61% Black and 2.8% Hispanic plus a 2.76% AIAN gap, AL-SD-5 has a meaningful non-white minority population that comprises over 12% of the district but can become invisible in a campaign focused entirely on the white homeowner majority. Economic mobility, healthcare access, and workforce opportunity are constituent concerns for Black and Hispanic Alabamians that exist independently of white homeowner economic security; treating these communities as secondary or derivative rather than as core constituencies with distinct needs and expectations risks both ethical governance failure and the practical electoral vulnerability of an opponent who builds more authentic minority community relationships." }
