          { name: "Renter Constituency Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 27.8% renter rate in AL-SD-9 represents approximately 7,000-8,000 households for whom housing affordability, wage floor, childcare costs, and job accessibility are material concerns that do not automatically align with homeowner-dominant priorities. Young adults and lower-income families in rental housing evaluate candidates on whether they are addressing the economic constraints that renters face — and an incumbent who speaks only to homeowner concerns risks losing the capacity to respond if a challenger emerges who speaks directly to renter economic anxiety." },
          { name: "AIAN Community Engagement and Governance Partnership", tag: "careful", why: "The 3.8% gap between the four reported Census categories and 100% represents approximately 2,000-2,500 residents in AL-SD-9 who are likely Native American constituents; while this is smaller than the AIAN population in other Alabama districts, it is large enough to be a meaningful constituency group. If tribal nations or Native American community organizations are present in the district, they have specific governance interests — healthcare access, economic development, and state recognition — that require proactive engagement rather than passive assumption; any emerging challenger could activate this constituency around specific issues if the incumbent has not built a genuine relationship." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Incumbency as Proof of Seat Security", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2022 election is not evidence of electoral dominance — it is an absence of opposition that can mask latent vulnerabilities. If a credible challenger emerges in the next cycle, the incumbent has no recent record of persuading opposition voters or demonstrating responsiveness to constituencies that are present but electorally quiet under current conditions; the 27.8% renter rate and 3.8% AIAN gap represent groups that could shift quickly if activated around specific grievances or policy promises." },
          { name: "Relying on Partisan Alignment Without Active Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "AL-SD-9's Republican alignment is real and durable at the homeowner-majority level, but it is not automatic for renters or AIAN constituents — these groups require active engagement to maintain loyalty or prevent defection. An incumbent who assumes that partisan registration or voting history will hold these constituencies without demonstrating responsiveness to their material concerns is operating on a false floor; an election without opposition does not provide feedback about whether that assumption is justified." }
