          { name: "Living Wage Employment and Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 23.1% college attainment and median income below state average, AL-SD-20's economic foundation is working-class employment in service, healthcare, logistics, or public sectors where wages stagnate and advancement pathways are limited. A representative who engages actively with workforce development organizations, community colleges, and employers on apprenticeship programs, living wage advocacy, and skills training is directly addressing the pathway through which working-age adults in the district build household economic stability. Legislative action on minimum wage, job training funding, or support for union organizing demonstrates tangible commitment to the wage and employment concerns that structure daily economic life for constituents." },
          { name: "Community Health Access and Preventive Care", tag: "careful", why: "Working-class households with limited educational attainment and below-average income face significant healthcare access barriers — both in terms of insurance affordability and geographic proximity to quality providers. AL-SD-20 likely has substantial Medicaid-eligible population, making healthcare policy and healthcare provider investment direct constituent concerns with material household impact. A representative who is visible with community health organizations and who demonstrates knowledge of healthcare access gaps in the district can build trust, but health policy engagement that becomes too technical or disconnected from the lived experience of uninsured or underinsured constituents can also read as institutional distance." },
          { name: "Presenting Unopposed Victory as Permanent Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2022 result reflects the absence of a challenger, not necessarily dominant constituent enthusiasm or deep incumbent security. In a heavily Democratic district, the actual electoral threat is not from the right but from a primary challenger or alternative who can credibly argue the incumbent has become unresponsive to working-class constituent economic concerns. Treating an unopposed result as a mandate for decreased constituent engagement or visibility opens the door to exactly the type of challenge that can succeed in a district where Democratic voters dominate." },
          { name: "Rhetorical Focus on Identity and History Without Material Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "A district that is 64.81% Black with strong Democratic alignment deserves authentic engagement on historical and ongoing racial equity concerns, but that engagement is only durable if paired with material economic delivery on the issues that structure working-age household stability: housing, wages, healthcare, and education. A representative whose public rhetoric emphasizes identity and history but whose legislative record on renter protection, living wage, or healthcare access is thin or absent will eventually face the criticism that they are performing representation rather than delivering it — and in a working-class district, that distinction is politically fatal." }
