          { name: "Treating 66% as a Permanent Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66-34 result is a strong Republican performance, but in a district with 37.8% renters, 31.7-year median age, and 31.6% college education, treating the margin as invulnerable to economic shocks or credible Democratic challenge is strategically dangerous. If housing costs accelerate, healthcare access becomes a crisis, or a Democratic nominee makes cost-of-living a central message, the 34% opposition in a young, rent-burdened district can mobilize and shift a narrow majority baseline into competitive territory. Confidence based on the 2022 result, without active constituent engagement and concrete policy responsiveness to non-homeowner concerns, is the path to vulnerability." },
          { name: "Assuming the Renter Constituency is Naturally Democratic", tag: "avoid", why: "While renters nationally lean Democratic, in a 66% Republican district the 37.8% renter population includes working-class families, young professionals, and others who may be persuadable on economic management, public safety, or education quality if the Republican candidate is responsive to their material concerns. A representative who dismisses renters as an automatic Democratic constituency, rather than engaging them on the specific economic pressures they face at $62,675 income in a high-cost San Diego context, forfeits the opportunity to build a cross-cutting coalition that makes the seat genuinely durable across demographic groups." }
