          { name: "Workforce Development and College Attainment as Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 22.2% college-attainment rate signals significant educational barriers for a working-age population (37.7 median age) competing in a labor market increasingly stratified by credentials. State investment in accessible community college pathways, apprenticeships, and targeted workforce training for high-wage occupations (healthcare, skilled trades) directly addresses the income constraint embedded in the $48,098 median household income. This is both a material constituent service and an authentic alignment between demographic reality and legislative priority." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Medicaid Expansion", tag: "careful", why: "In a district with significant Black population, working-age majority, and $48,098 median income, healthcare costs are a direct household budget pressure and a barrier to economic stability. Medicaid expansion and removal of administrative barriers to healthcare access have demonstrated material impact on constituent health and financial security. However, this issue also intersects with state political constraints and federal policy that limit what a state senator can unilaterally deliver, requiring careful framing of what is actionable versus aspirational." },
          { name: "Treating an 82% Margin as Ideological Mandate Rather Than Constituent Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "Supermajority margins rest on demonstrated alignment between a representative's legislative priorities and constituents' material economic concerns. A representative who interprets the 82% result as permission to prioritize abstract ideological positioning over concrete constituent service on housing, wages, and healthcare is misreading the mandate. Loyalty at this scale is contingent; erosion begins when constituents perceive that the representative has stopped fighting for their economic security in favor of partisan theater." },
          { name: "Assuming Supermajority Stability Without Active Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "An 82% margin in a heavily Democratic district is not a permanent condition — it is the product of demonstrated constituent service and alignment with the material concerns that structure daily life for renters, working-age adults, and families at $48,098 income. A representative who treats this margin as a floor rather than an achievement, and who relies on partisan loyalty rather than active constituent engagement on housing, wages, and healthcare, is vulnerable to primary challenge from a candidate who offers more concrete delivery on economic security. The 82% is durable only through sustained demonstration that the representative is fighting for constituent interests on the issues that matter most." }
