          { name: "Workforce Development and Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 16.7% college attainment rate and $44,634 income together signal a constituency where credible pathways to wage growth—apprenticeships, technical training, union job access, and wage-floor policy—are the primary mechanism for economic improvement. Legislative action on paid family leave, childcare subsidies, job training funding, and wage standards directly affect whether working-age families with children in this district experience material economic progress. These issues bind Black, white, and Hispanic constituents around shared economic interests rather than separating them into demographic silos." },
          { name: "AIAN Community Presence and Healthcare Access", tag: "careful", why: "The 3.3% demographic gap in a district of 53% Black and 39% white represents American Indian/Alaska Native constituents whose presence in urban San Diego may be less visible than tribal-majority districts but whose healthcare, economic, and cultural community needs are real and present. In a heavily Democratic district, AIAN constituents are unlikely to swing to the opposition, but neglecting active engagement and specific policy responsiveness around healthcare access through Indian Health Service frameworks or tribal economic development can suppress turnout or energy when it matters for close state or federal races." },
          { name: "Treating 81% as a Secure Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "An 81% Democratic result in an urban district can create complacency in which the incumbent assumes the base is consolidated and does not require active service delivery, material results, or constituent responsiveness. In fact, 81% in a working-class urban district reflects successful consolidation of a coalition that evaluates primarily on whether government materially improves their lives—housing costs, jobs, and services. An incumbent who campaigns as if 81% is secure while failing to deliver on these material concerns creates space for internal Democratic opposition or low turnout in higher-order races." },
          { name: "Assuming Party Loyalty Substitutes for Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "Working-class urban constituencies do not give loyalty based on party affiliation alone—they give votes based on whether they experience government as responsive and materially helpful. An incumbent who rests on partisan dominance without visible investment in constituent services, housing solutions, job-quality improvement, or childcare access will find that 81% erodes when an alternative voice credibly argues that government is failing to serve working-class needs. In a district with these economic fundamentals, constituent delivery is not optional for holding the seat." }
