          "The most significant strategic vulnerability is demographic: the four reported Census categories account for approximately 95% of the district's population, leaving a ~5% gap that likely represents American Indian, Alaska Native, or unidentified populations. While this gap is smaller than in tribal-concentrated districts, it is not negligible in a district this prosperous and stable, and any credible future challenger will test whether the incumbent has built authentic relationships with all constituency communities. The 2022 unopposed victory also creates an incumbent risk: the absence of contested election forces means no recent data on turnout, persuadion, or emerging issues. An incumbent who rests on 2022's result without actively building relationships, identifying emerging constituency concerns, and demonstrating responsiveness to all demographic communities runs the risk of being surprised by a well-organized challenger in the next competitive cycle, particularly if economic conditions deteriorate or local governance failures emerge."
