          { name: "Healthcare Access and Cost Burden", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 23.9% college attainment and $45,893 median income indicate limited health insurance coverage, high out-of-pocket healthcare costs, and pharmacy access constraints for a substantial portion of the district. Medicaid expansion implementation, prescription drug cost reduction, community clinic access improvement, and maternal health equity are the healthcare governance priorities most directly responsive to the district's population. A representative's ability to secure measurable improvement in these areas is a primary determinant of whether the 67% Democratic margin holds or contracts in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Racial and Ethnic Minority Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "While 64.03% Black, the district includes 30.43% white, 2.61% Hispanic, and 0.88% Asian residents, plus approximately 2% likely AIAN — meaning 35-36% of the constituency is not part of the Black majority. These minority communities face the same housing affordability, healthcare access, and economic mobility concerns as the Black majority but can become invisible in district governance framed primarily around racial equity for the Black community. Specific, visible engagement with white, Hispanic, and Asian constituents — not token representation, but genuine program attention — prevents these communities from becoming mobilized by opposition candidates claiming they are being ignored." },
          { name: "Assuming the 67% Is a Durable Floor Rather Than an Electoral Mandate to Deliver", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67-33 margin in a majority-Black urban district reflects a working-class constituency voting based on direct economic necessity, not inherited partisan loyalty. The margin is contingent on a representative's demonstrated capacity to improve housing affordability, healthcare access, and wage standards — material concerns that do not improve automatically with Democratic governance. Any incumbent or challenger who treats 67% as a safe baseline rather than a score to be earned in each cycle through specific delivery on these concerns will discover the margin is more fragile than it appears." },
          { name: "Treating Minority Communities as Peripheral Rather Than Central Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "A district that is 64% Black but 36% other racial and ethnic groups requires integrated governance, not segregated attention where the white, Hispanic, and Asian minorities are treated as an afterthought or absorbed into a generic 'people of color' framing. In a working-class urban district where all residents face the same material constraints, framing governance around racial identity rather than shared economic concerns can create divisions where coalition is possible. This risks opening pathways for opposition candidates to argue they offer better service to communities that feel neglected by an incumbent focused narrowly on the Black majority." }
